<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            A quad remesher: a triangle soup becomes an all-quad mesh with clean edge loops, hard
            features reproduced exactly because they are layout boundaries rather than something
            snapped to afterwards, and the source's attributes carried across. Seven stages, each one
            an inspectable artefact, and the same input produces the same bytes on any thread count.
            Arithmetic over triangles with no model, no native dependency and no device — which is
            what lets a content build run it.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>geometry;retopology;remesh;quad;mesh;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
            ⚠ Optimised even in Debug, for the reason Vixen.Geometry.Uv records at length: the field
            solve and the extractor are float inner loops, and unoptimised they are what makes a
            local run of this suite 327 s against CI's 85 s. Measured back to back on one machine:
            Debug 327 s, Debug with this property 87 s, Release 85 s, 340 tests passing in each.
            `DEBUG` is still defined, so nothing that reads `JobScheduler.SafetyChecksEnabled` or
            `LeakTracker.IsSupported` changes behaviour.
        -->
        <Optimize Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">true</Optimize>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            docs/plan/41 § D2, amended by docs/plan/42 § D2 for the fourth entry.

            ⚠ `Vixen.Core.Threading` is not free — it carries `Vixen.Core` and
            `Vixen.Core.Diagnostics`, so the closure is six assemblies rather than the four named
            here. `RemeshingLayeringTests` names all six.

            ⚠ The reference to `Vixen.Geometry.Uv` is one-way and doc 42 § D2 is why: the remesher
            keeps its privileged charting, because a quantized quad patch *is* a rectangle and
            re-cutting it with a general charter would be worse in every respect — but it stops
            owning a second packer. One packer, one margin rule.

            Not referenced, and the absences are the design: no graphics, no assets, no editor. The
            four callers that are not the editor (the asset compiler, the CLI, the blockout mode and
            a game's own tooling) are why this is `Core/` and not `Editor/`, which is the one place
            this document parts company with docs/plan/40 § D9 — and it parts company because that
            section's reasons are all statements about *models*, and there is no model here.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Geometry\Vixen.Geometry.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Geometry.Uv\Vixen.Geometry.Uv.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
